# Hugpy Core

Core is the Flask console, API and fleet coordinator. It registers Wrapper hosts,
mirrors their execution evidence into PostgreSQL, reads the model inventory
Discovery publishes, and routes work.
Wrapper owns execution, loading, memory admission, worker settings and local model
copies. Core uses Wrapper's reported feasibility; it does not run a second sizing
solver. See [model data ownership](docs/MODEL_DATA_OWNERSHIP.md) and the
[fleet execution direction](docs/FLEET_EXECUTION_CONTRACT.md).

The package metadata defines the source version. Check the installed distribution
and running service separately when diagnosing an installation.

## Start and install

With the package and its dependencies installed and a prepared Core PostgreSQL
DSN in `HUGPY_CORE_DB`, the HTTP entry point is:

```sh
hugpy-core serve --host 127.0.0.1 --port 7020
```

Startup checks migrations and initializes Core services; use an isolated database
for development. `serve` uses the threaded Werkzeug server. A bare `hugpy-core`
uses the older Flask startup sequence with additional startup jobs.

Core depends on Storage, Engine, Terminal, Resources, Settings, Abstract
Toolserver and the shared Flask/database/utilities libraries as well as Flask,
aiohttp, psycopg and Flask-Sock. It does not import Wrapper or Discovery: it
reaches the Wrapper over its HTTP front door and reads Discovery's published
export (`hugpy_core.discovery`), per the one-way discovery → wrapper → core
ownership rule. Exact floors and optional extras are in [pyproject.toml](pyproject.toml).
Media and Video are optional: their distributions own their APIs and UI assets.
Toolserver provisions databases; Core queries PostgreSQL directly.

## Media and Video on the same host

Media (`hugpy-media`) and Video (`hugpy-video`) are separate packages, each able to run without
Core as its own service. On a host that also has Core they are served by Core, in-process, at
`/media/` and `/video/`. Nothing has to be configured and the order of installation does not
matter:

```sh
sudo apt-get install hugpy-core hugpy-media hugpy-video    # any subset, in any order, now or later
hugpy-core addon list                                      # media: embedded / video: embedded
```

Each arm's package ships a descriptor in `/usr/share/hugpy/core-addons.d/`. When the arm is
installed it asks Core to take it (`hugpy-core addon attach <name>`); when Core is installed or
upgraded it takes every arm described on the box (`hugpy-core addon sync`). Taking an arm means
installing its wheels into Core's environment from the arm's own offline wheel directory, with
every distribution Core already has pinned to its installed version: the arm is added and nothing
Core was installed with is replaced. The arm's standalone service is then switched off.

An arm Core cannot hold that way (it needs a newer sibling than this Core carries) is left
untouched on its own service, and Core reaches it through its `arms.d` descriptor instead, so
the arm still appears under Core's URLs. Removing the arm's package takes it back out of Core;
removing Core returns the arm to its own service.

`HUGPY_CORE_ADDONS` in Core's `core.env` decides what may be embedded: unset or `auto` (every
described arm), `none` (arms stay separate services behind Core), or a comma list of names.
Run `sudo hugpy-core addon sync` after changing it.

With pip instead of Debian packages, the same result is one environment holding both:
`pip install "hugpy-core[media,video]"` (or `hugpy-media[core]`, `hugpy-video[core]`).

## Who needs an API key

`/v1/*` is the model API, `/api/*` the control surface. Core checks keys on `/v1` and on
`/live-api`; `/api` carries no key check of its own (a front gates it with the operator's
session, and direct callers are whoever can reach Core's bind address).

Those three prefixes, `/health`, and the console's own files and pages are everything Core
serves. Any other path is a 404 with a JSON body naming the prefixes: a control route called
without `/api` (`/llm/workers`, `/models`) does not return the console page. A page below its
entry point (`/console/workers`, `/video/jobs/7`) is returned to a browser navigation only
(`Accept: text/html`); a program asking for it gets the same 404. A client's base
is the bare origin; it never ends in `/api` or `/v1`. `/api/v1/*` is the model API as the
console's signed-in session reaches it through a front; clients with a key use `/v1`.

The requirement has three states, set from the console's API access switch or
`POST /api/keys/require`:

| mode | who needs a key on `/v1` and `/live-api` |
|---|---|
| `never` | nobody (the switch off) |
| `always` | everybody, callers on this box included (the switch on) |
| `outside` | callers from outside this box and its trusted networks; local callers do not. Only `{"mode": "outside"}` selects it; it is also what an install whose switch was never used runs with |

A caller that reaches Core through a reverse proxy is judged by the last address in
`X-Forwarded-For` that is not one of Core's proxies. Core trusts no proxy until
`HUGPY_ROUTER_TRUSTED_PROXIES` names it (for a front on the same host: `127.0.0.1,::1`), so
until then every forwarded caller needs a key. Set it only for a front that writes
`X-Forwarded-For` itself on every location it sends to Core. `HUGPY_ROUTER_TRUSTED_NETS`
replaces the networks that count as local (loopback and the private ranges).

`/live-api/<section>/<path>` is `/api/<section>/<path>` for a key holder: the same routes,
behind the same rule as `/v1`, limited to the sections in `hugpy_core.web.ui.LIVE_SECTIONS`
(models, llm, chat, prompt, session, uploads, media, video, movie, oracle, version, fleet,
agent). `fleet` and `agent` are open to a use key only in part (`LIVE_USE_ROUTES`: the worker
tree and the model and catalog reads; a node's `agent/register`); the rest of those two
sections needs an admin key. A client
that holds a key uses a bare origin as its base, `/v1` for model calls and `/live-api` for
control calls, whether it talks to a front or straight to Core. Key management, settings and
the other operator surfaces are not reachable that way.

`hugpy_core.web.api_access.principal()` answers "who is calling" for the current request
(`scope` admin, use or none; `inside`; whether it came through a front's session gate). Arms
Core hosts receive it with Core's other services.

## API, UI and operations

- [Operator guide](docs/OPERATOR-GUIDE.md): configuration, intake, optional apps,
  Identity readiness and verification boundaries.
- [Documentation index](docs/README.md): current contracts and historical designs.
- [Route convention](docs/ROUTES-CONVENTION.md): category catalogs, explicit addon
  blueprints and UI middleware. `/routes` is not an exhaustive endpoint inventory.
- [Console source and component API](../ui/README.md): app and library builds.
- [Intake deployment](../installers/deploy/INTAKE.md): independent collection and
  bounded model-fact exchange between registered workers.

The retired Basic aiohttp server, console and training extension are preserved
in [archive/basic-runtime](../archive/basic-runtime/), outside the installable
package. The active Flask application uses native Core readers; the compatibility
flag and Basic CLI dispatch were removed. Existing databases are not migrated or
deleted merely by this source change.

